## Introduction 

A recent article by @Lutz:2026, "Statistical sleuths versus AI: Unmasking the killer in Agatha Christie
novels" in *Significance* 23(6), caught my eye.  I've always been a
fan of Agatha Christie's books, and the idea of using AI to solve murder mysteries appealed to me.

What would Hercule Poirot be able to do with a few extra "little grey cells", 
the phrase he used to emphasize the skills of logical deduction, psychology, and quiet mental analysis over 
merely physical evidence or external clues, of the kind pursued by more hide-bound detectives of popular
fiction? 

Whether or not modern AI tools can be useful more generally in the analysis of detective fiction or whether
it will just spoil the fun of reading is a question I leave for others to ponder.

### Lutz analysis

Lutz coded 166 main characters in the 12 Miss Marple
novels (14 killers) by gender, employment, relation to the victim, and financial gain, and
added three AI "guesses" (Harvard AI Sandbox, GPT 5.1/5.6) based on the opening of each
novel. Her question was essentially "Could a LLM become a consulting detective?"

This document reproduces her analysis, corrects the odds ratios in her Table 1, and
extends it with multivariable and conditional (within-novel) logistic models.
It also gives me a chance to try out some of my graphical methods for categorical data analysis
in the [`vcd`](https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=vcd) [@R-vcd],
[`vcdExtra`](https://friendly.github.io/vcdExtra/) [@R-vcdExtra], and
[`ggfourfold`](https://gavinklorfine.com/ggfourfold/) [@R-ggfourfold] packages.

Only the code for the key models is shown here. All of the code, including the figures,
is in [`AgathaChristie.R`](R/AgathaChristie.R) and [`figures.R`](R/figures.R).


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="true" code-summary="Code: Read the data"}
library(survival)   # clogit()
library(pROC)       # auc()
library(vcd)        # oddsratio()
library(vcdExtra)

dat <- read.csv("AgathaChristie_MissMarple_MainCharacters_v2.csv",
                fileEncoding = "UTF-8-BOM")
dat$Title <- factor(dat$Title)
dat$Novel <- factor(gsub("_", " ", dat$Title))

preds    <- c("Female", "Employed", "Related", "Financial")
ai_preds <- c("AIch1passage", "AIch1web", "AIch20p")
labels   <- c(Female = "Female", Employed = "Employed", Related = "Related to victim",
              Financial = "Financial gain", AIch1passage = "AI: Ch.1 pasted",
              AIch1web = "AI: Ch.1 from web", AIch20p = "AI: ~20% pasted")

# factor copies for tables & plots
fac <- dat
for (v in c("Killer", preds, ai_preds))
  fac[[v]] <- factor(dat[[v]], levels = 0:1, labels = c("No", "Yes"))

# draw to the knitr device inside Quarto, otherwise save a PNG in figures/
in_knitr <- isTRUE(getOption("knitr.in.progress"))
if (!in_knitr) dir.create("figures", showWarnings = FALSE)
savefig <- function(name, expr, width = 7, height = 6) {
  if (!in_knitr) {
    png(file.path("figures", paste0(name, ".png")), width = width, height = height,
        units = "in", res = 150)
    on.exit(dev.off())
  }
  expr
  invisible()
}

aucval <- function(fit) as.numeric(auc(dat$Killer, fitted(fit), quiet = TRUE))
```
:::


## The data

Lutz's data, from her [GitHub repository](https://github.com/SharonLutz/Stats-vs-AI-whodunnit),
have one row per character, with the novel's title and 0/1 indicators for being the
killer, the four character traits, and the three AI guesses. The setup code above reads
them and makes No/Yes factor copies for tables and plots. A copy of the data file is here:
[`AgathaChristie_MissMarple_MainCharacters_v2.csv`](AgathaChristie_MissMarple_MainCharacters_v2.csv).

The output below gives the counts, the percentage of characters with each trait (or
flagged by each AI guess), and a three-way table of Killer by Related and Financial gain,
the two traits that matter most.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="true" code-summary="Code: Quick summary"}
cat(nrow(dat), "characters,", sum(dat$Killer), "killers,", nlevels(dat$Title), "novels\n")
cat("Percent of characters\n")
cat("\nCharacter traits\n")
round(100 * colMeans(dat[, c("Killer", preds)]), 1)
cat("\nAI guesses\n")
round(100 * colMeans(dat[, ai_preds]), 1)
```

