A difference in proportions lies between −1 and 1, so its exponential can never be
smaller than 1/e ≈ 0.37 or larger than e ≈ 2.72, however strong the association. Her AUCs
are not affected: with a single binary predictor, every model ranks the characters the
same way. Her *p*-values, also from the linear model, are much smaller than the logistic
ones below, but they put the predictors in the same order. It is the odds ratios
themselves that are wrong.

### Multivariable AUCs

The AUC (area under the ROC curve) is the probability that a randomly chosen killer gets a
higher fitted score than a randomly chosen innocent character: 0.5 is chance, 1 is
perfect. Here it is computed with the `pROC` package [@Robin-etal:2011:pROC]. Her four multivariable models add each AI guess, in turn, to Related and
Financial gain. Fitting them as logistic regressions, her AUCs (0.89, 0.89, 0.97, 0.92)
reproduce too:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="true" code-summary="Code: AUCs for Lutz's multivariable models"}
# Lutz's four multivariable models (AUC only is reported; AUC is the same for logit or LPM
# only when the fitted values have the same ordering, so use the logistic fits)
lutz_models <- list(
  "Related + Gain"            = Killer ~ Related + Financial,
  "Related + Gain + AI ch1"   = Killer ~ Related + Financial + AIch1passage,
  "Related + Gain + AI web"   = Killer ~ Related + Financial + AIch1web,
  "Related + Gain + AI 20%"   = Killer ~ Related + Financial + AIch20p)
lutz_fits <- lapply(lutz_models, glm, family = binomial, data = dat)
data.frame(AUC  = round(sapply(lutz_fits, aucval), 3),
           Lutz = c(0.89, 0.89, 0.97, 0.92))
```

