## Caveats

* 14 killers in 12 novels: models with more than two or three predictors overfit, and
  in-sample AUCs and hit rates are optimistic (see the cross-validation above). With 12
  folds, the CV estimates themselves are noisy.
* Lutz notes that the "web" AI condition performed better than giving the AI the same text
  directly, which suggests it was drawing on outside knowledge of these famous books. Its
  advantage may be partly leakage, not reading.

## What I learned

The reanalysis gives Miss Marple good reason to follow the money. The corrected odds
ratio puts the odds of being the killer at about 52 times as high for characters who
stood to gain. 

When it comes to choosing one suspect in a **held-out novel**, family ties
and financial gain give a 68% hit rate. The web-based AI guess brings that to 76%,
and it flags both killers among the characters with neither coded motive. For those
two cases, the mystery is what the AI knew about the ending when it made its guess.

So, could an LLM become a consulting detective? On this evidence, it would make a useful
assistant, but not yet a Miss Marple. On its own, its best guess (web Chapter 1) names
the killer 53% of the time, a little below simply following the money (57%). Its value
comes as one more clue added to motive and family ties. 

And the version that helped
most was the one that could look things up, not the one that read the same chapter. A
consulting detective reasons from the clues on the page; with books this famous, the AI
may simply have remembered how they end. Poirot's "little grey cells" would want a
fairer test: mysteries the AI could not have read.

### What was missing?

More generally, I thought of what was missing in the data used here, that would give Lutz's
question and framework for analysis wider scope and interest, in both literary analysis
of detective fiction and the use of AI for this endeavor:

* **Complete text**: I understand why Lutz chose three different indicators of AI classification based on different criteria for what the AI model read. I find this interesting as a technique. But a better test of AI 
would be to use the complete text of these books for comparison.

* **What's missing?**: What other variables could be coded? Lutz's four traits are easy
to code from a character list, but they cover only one kind of motive (money) and one
kind of relationship (family). Christie's killers also act out of love, jealousy,
revenge, or to hide a secret from the past, so a richer set of motive codes would help.
The classic triad of *means, motive and opportunity* suggests others: who had access to
the poison, who had no alibi. And there are features of the telling rather than the
characters, such as when each character first appears, how much of the book they
occupy, and whether the narrative casts suspicion on them early. With only 14 killers,
of course, any of these would have to be traded off against the risk of overfitting;
adding the Poirot novels would help.

* **Understanding Agatha Christie**: British mystery writers of the era of Agatha Christie had a formula to
captivate their readers: create a scenario involving a murder, tell the story of the characters,
planting clues & red herrings along the way. Enter the detective to the story in some way and
end with a scene in which all the suspects are assembled together, and the detective points his
finger at the killer. The game was meant to be played fairly. Members of the Detection
Club, Christie among them, swore not to withhold vital clues from the reader
[@Edwards:2015]. Within
those rules, Christie's skill lay in misdirection, and she was willing to bend the
rules themselves: the narrator is the killer in *The Murder of Roger Ackroyd*, and in
*Murder on the Orient Express* nearly every suspect is [@Crampton:2019]. Static traits like "related"
and "gains financially" capture what a careful reader could deduce, but not how
Christie steers the reader away from it. Coding the story as it unfolds, such as which
characters fall under suspicion chapter by chapter and when the decisive clue is
planted, would let us ask how she does it. Most importantly, it would also give a fairer test for an AI:
could it follow the clues as a reader does, and not just recall the ending?

